# First: Why Do We Need Special Schemas?

In OLTP databases (banking, college systems), we normalize data.

Example:

```text
Students
Courses
Departments
Faculty
```

Many tables.

This is good for transactions.

---

But in analytics, managers ask questions like:

```text
Total sales by city?

Top products this year?

Revenue by month?
```

We need queries to be:

✔ Fast
✔ Easy to understand

This is where Star and Snowflake schemas help.

---

# 1️⃣ Star Schema

## Big Idea

A Star Schema has:

```text
One Fact Table
+
Multiple Dimension Tables
```

---

## Mental Model 🧠

Think of the sun.

```text
          Product
             |
Customer -- Sales -- Time
             |
          Location
```

Looks like a star ⭐

---

# Fact Table

The Fact Table stores:

```text
Measurements
Numbers
Business events
```

Example:

### Sales Fact Table

| Product_ID | Customer_ID | Time_ID | Amount |
| ---------- | ----------- | ------- | ------ |
| 101        | 1           | 500     | 2000   |

---

Contains:

```text
Sales Amount
Quantity
Revenue
Profit
```

---

# Dimension Tables

Provide descriptive information.

---

### Product Dimension

| Product_ID | Product_Name |
| ---------- | ------------ |
| 101        | Laptop       |

---

### Customer Dimension

| Customer_ID | Customer_Name |
| ----------- | ------------- |
| 1           | Akshay        |

---

### Time Dimension

| Time_ID | Month |
| ------- | ----- |
| 500     | April |

---

# Why Star Schema Is Fast

Suppose manager asks:

```text
Total laptop sales in April?
```

Fact table contains numbers.

Dimension tables provide details.

Query becomes simple.

---

# Mental Model 🧠

```text
Fact = Numbers

Dimension = Description
```

---

# Memory Trick

```text
Star Schema

One center table

Many surrounding tables
```

---

# Python Analogy 🧠

Think:

```python
sales = [
 {
   "product":"Laptop",
   "amount":2000
 }
]
```

Fact stores transaction.

Dimensions store extra information.

---

# Advantages

✔ Fast queries

✔ Easy reporting

✔ Easy understanding

---

# Disadvantage

Some data redundancy exists.

---

# 2️⃣ Snowflake Schema

Now let's improve Star Schema.

---

# Problem

Dimension tables can become huge.

Example:

### Product Table

| Product | Category    | Department |
| ------- | ----------- | ---------- |
| Laptop  | Electronics | Technology |

Category repeats many times.

---

# Solution

Normalize dimension tables.

---

# Product Dimension

| Product_ID | Product_Name | Category_ID |
| ---------- | ------------ | ----------- |

---

# Category Dimension

| Category_ID | Category |
| ----------- | -------- |

---

# Department Dimension

| Department_ID | Department |
| ------------- | ---------- |

---

Now schema looks like:

```text
          Category
              |
Product ---- Sales ---- Time
              |
          Customer
```

More branches.

Looks like a snowflake ❄️

---

# Mental Model 🧠

```text
Star Schema
=
Simple

Snowflake Schema
=
Normalized Star
```

---

# Easy Comparison

| Feature       | Star            | Snowflake       |
| ------------- | --------------- | --------------- |
| Structure     | Simple          | More tables     |
| Normalization | Low             | High            |
| Query Speed   | Faster          | Slightly slower |
| Storage       | More redundancy | Less redundancy |
| Complexity    | Easy            | Complex         |

---

# Real Interview Question

### Which is Faster?

👉 Star Schema

Why?

Because:

```text
Fewer joins
```

---

### Which Uses Less Storage?

👉 Snowflake Schema

Why?

Because:

```text
Less redundancy
```

---

